<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Burma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> is the southern, coastal part of Myanmar, centred on the Irrawaddy delta and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>. It was long the land of Mon kingdoms such as Hanthawaddy, often at war with the Burman rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> of 1852, and under British rule the delta was cleared for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, becoming one of the world's leading rice-exporting regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lower-burma.jpg" alt="Lower Burma" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Al Bagania · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_Geopolitical_Regions_of_Myanmar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After that the kingdom was landlocked and dependent on river trade through British-held <b>Lower Burma</b>, and Mindon sought new revenue through royal monopolies and the thathameda, a household tax.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…became independent as the Union of Burma on 4 January 1948, ending more than six decades of British rule that had begun with the annexation of Arakan and Tenasserim in 1826 and of <b>Lower Burma</b> in 1852 and the fall of the Konbaung kingdom at Mandalay in 1885.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His rejection of Yuan demands for tribute brought defeat at Ngasaunggyan in 1277 and the loss of the north in the 1280s; he fled to <b>Lower Burma</b>, earning the name ‘the king who fled from the Chinese’, and was murdered by his son Thihathu at Prome in 1287.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…(r. 1550–1581) had built the largest empire in the history of mainland Southeast Asia, but under his son Nanda Bayin it disintegrated: Siam broke free, <b>Lower Burma</b> was depopulated by war and famine, and in 1599 the capital Pegu was sacked by the forces of Toungoo and Arakan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His kingdom, later called Hanthawaddy, ruled <b>Lower Burma</b> until 1539.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Second Anglo-Burmese War in 1852 added <b>Lower Burma</b> with Rangoon.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-burma">British Burma</a></li></ul></section>
